Z||000000||Predator||04-23-2001||09:01 AM||||Hey I have a 1985 Fiero GT Turbo. I am running lean at wide open throttle. I was wondering if anyone has tried using the cold start injector, or seventh injector to be used as an extra injector to kick in at higher RPMS? Any ideas would be appreciated. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||BBBAD GT||04-23-2001||09:12 AM||||I would suggest a rising rate fuel pressure regulator, or an external FMU with adjustablility.  I have a Vortech Super FMU on my blown 5.0, and the fuel pressure rises along with boost until it hits around 68psi of fuel pressure.  <P>These are external ways of correcting a lean condition, but I would say if you had a custom chip done (did it come with the kit), then you could tune the pulse width of the injector to stay on longer at WOT say &gt;4000 rpms.  You might have to step up to bigger injectors and a custom chip.  <P>What kind of shape is your fuel pump in (and how big)?<P><P>------------------<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero/V8sig.jpg">        <P><A HREF="http://home.att.net/~nomercy5.0/V8Specs.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8 For Sale</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||04-23-2001||11:29 AM||||jeff he's got a NASTY lean spike at from 2800-4500 it's HARSH.. he's running a custom chip from the design one people and they are suposed to be checking into it... but thats been going on for some time. he's ok off idle to 2500 staying in the high 12s (12.5-12.8) and dropeds in tot he 15s in the the worst of his lean-ness think an SFMU would redidie his problem? if so we've got some at the shop... Donald e-mail jeff your dyno-air/fuel graph if you've got it handy. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Predator||04-23-2001||11:54 AM||||Thanks for the replys. I will email you the Dyno run when I get home Jeff. Travis, I really need to get this fixed. I have tried Kevin at Design One a couple of times and have had no luck. I understand that I did not buy it directly from him so I am trying to cut him some slack on figuring out why I am having this problem. I kind of figured he would like to be involved since it is his kit, and he could incorporate the fix into future kits. Anywho, I need a cheap fix if there is one, if not whatever it takes. I can't afford to blow this motor with all the money I already have invested in it. Thanks for all the help. Stock fuel pump, regulator, and injectors. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||1FST2M6||04-23-2001||01:22 PM||||changing the injectors i may help.. the added fuel pressure.. may help a bit but i don't think you'll gain NEARLY as much as you've lost already in the mid range. it's weird that it's fine in the upper RPM range and TOAST in the mid range... that much of a dip isn't and easy fix..  i'm betting that he hasn't had anyone really test his kits like Ed did. and the design flaw is in all the kits. i'll ask Ed if an RPM activated and throttle position switch could activate the cold start for ya.. <P>LOl.. oh i found out how mu an AIC is.. $600 your cost... Nopi carries it for over $800 plus installation, and the rest of the crap easily pushing you over the $1000 mark.. so i didn't even bother to e-mail the price when i found out... i was just to busy laughin.. "..you want HOW MUCH for that..." anywho... i'll keep watching this post and see what Jeff comes up with. he was able to show me the error of my SEs fuel system (running 100+psi through 30# injectors was giving me hella torque on nitrous but oof the squeeze 6mpg sucked. he had me drop my pressure down to 60psi and i gained HP and didn't kill my torque either and that bummped me up to 14mpg!! it does help to know somone with a clue. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cooter||04-23-2001||03:30 PM||||Eventually I will be running my car off a 727 ecm with a GMC Syclone prom. It uses a knock sensor, two bar map and takes the boost into account when mapping the fuel. Of course, a special thanks to the Forum's resident chip guru for making it all possible!||||reg||1
